const QUIZ = [
  ["Why does an LLM hallucinate even on facts it was trained on?","It never stored facts � it learned statistical patterns of plausible continuations. Any prompt elicits plausible-sounding text; truth is only correlated with plausibility, never guaranteed."],
  ["Why is temperature 0 preferred for engineering tasks?","It makes sampling near-deterministic, so outputs are reproducible, testable, and debuggable � essential for evals and production reliability."],
  ["What do you actually send on every API call, and why?","The full message history � the model is stateless. The API stores nothing between calls; memory is your code re-sending the conversation each time."],
  ["When should you NOT hard-code an API key in your script?","Always. Keys belong in environment variables (.env + .gitignore) � a committed key is a leaked key, even in a 'private' repo."],
  ["In one line: what does cosine similarity measure?","The angle between two embedding vectors � ignoring magnitude, so two texts about the same meaning score close to 1 regardless of length."],
  ["Name the three chunking strategies and one tradeoff of each.","Fixed (simple, cuts sentences), recursive (respects structure, still arbitrary), semantic (meaning-based, slowest to compute)."],
  ["What goes in a weekly learning log?","Learned / built / stuck / next � five minutes every weekend; it converts experience into retention."],
  ["What is HyDE, in plain words?","Generate a hypothetical answer with the LLM first, then embed THAT and search � it bridges the vocabulary gap between questions and documents."],
  ["Why store vectors with metadata, not just raw text?","Metadata enables filtering (by date, source, permission, type) before similarity search � critical for accuracy and RBAC."],
  ["Why does BM25 still beat pure vectors on some queries?","Exact keyword/identifier matching � error codes, names, SKUs � where semantic similarity misses literal matches."],
  ["What does a re-ranker do that retrieval cannot?","It re-scores query?chunk pairs jointly (cross-encoder), catching subtle relevance mistakes the fast bi-encoder made."],
  ["Your RAG says 'I don't know' too often � what are the first 3 dials to turn?","top-k (retrieve more), chunk size/overlap (context too thin), similarity threshold (too strict)."],
  ["What does recall@k actually prove?","That the correct chunk was retrieved within the top k � it measures retrieval, not generation quality."],
  ["Why must you record yourself for FDE-style discovery calls?","You notice your own gaps � skipped questions, leading questions, no success metric � which are invisible in the moment."],
  ["Explain an agent to a skeptic using one sentence.","An agent is an LLM in a loop with tools, a budget, and a stop condition � it plans, acts, observes, repeats."],
  ["Why does MCP exist?","One standard protocol for any agent to use any tool server � no bespoke glue per application; like USB-C for AI tools."],
  ["What is an idempotency key and which tools need one?","A unique client-generated ID the server dedupes � required for any retried side-effecting action (charge, create ticket, send email)."],
  ["Prompt injection: what is the 'tool hijack' variant?","Malicious instructions inside retrieved data telling the agent to call your tools dangerously (delete, exfiltrate) � defense: allow-lists + HITL."],
  ["Name two stop conditions every production agent needs.","Max iterations and a token/cost budget � plus human escalation when exceeded."],
  ["Why are trajectory evals different from final-answer evals?","The right answer reached via a dangerous path (wrong tool, no approval) still failed � trajectory evals grade the process, not just the outcome."],
  ["What does a golden set need besides happy paths?","Unanswerable questions and adversarial inputs � or your metrics lie to you."],
  ["You must trust LLM-as-judge. What's mandatory before shipping?","Calibration against human labels (e.g., 10+ cases) and documented agreement � otherwise it's a random number generator with confidence."],
  ["Phoenix vs LangSmith � one honest difference.","Phoenix is open-source/self-hosted (data stays yours); LangSmith is managed with deeper evaluation tooling. Pick per data-sensitivity needs."],
  ["Why is semantic caching dangerous without invalidation?","A cached answer for a similar-but-not-identical query can serve wrong or stale content confidently."],
  ["Name the three prompt-injection defenses you'd deploy first.","Instruction/data delimiters, output validation, and least-privilege tool allow-lists."],
  ["Why does your eval suite belong in CI, not a notebook?","Notebooks get skipped under deadline pressure; CI blocks merges when quality regresses � that's the difference between demo and product."],
  ["You'll demo live to a customer. Name three things to prepare.","Backup environment + offline demo recording, rehearsed failure fallback, and a seeded dataset with known-good outputs."],
  ["Why write the success metric BEFORE building?","If the metric isn't agreed up front, 'done' is negotiated forever � and the customer always moves the goalposts."],
  ["What makes a flagship README different from a normal one?","It's written for strangers: problem in one line, architecture diagram, quickstart in 5 commands, eval table with numbers."],
  ["How many layers of testing should the demo have?","Three: unit tests for tools, eval suite for quality, and a scripted smoke test against the public URL after deploy."],
  ["What belongs in a production runbook?","Top-5 failures, each with: symptom, diagnosis, fix, escalation path � written before the first incident, updated after."],
  ["Why must the demo video show eval numbers, not just features?","Numbers are the only proof a non-engineer stakeholder trusts; adjectives are indistinguishable from marketing."],
  ["FDE: who is 'the customer' in your portfolio project?","Whomever you pretend to serve � and you should interview a real human in that role to make the requirements real."],
  ["Why two resume variants instead of one good one?","Same work framed two ways: depth (AI Engineer: architecture, evals, cost) vs outcome (FDE: adoption, rollout, metrics)."],
  ["What's the realistic conversion math for applications?","~50 applications ? ~10 screens ? ~3 onsites ? 1 offer. Volume matters, but tailoring multiplies it."],
  ["In enterprise RAG design, what does 'RBAC' mean for answers?","Retrieval must filter by the asker's permissions � users should never see chunks their role can't access, even via the LLM."],
  ["When asked 'why not just prompt the model?', what's your design-thinking answer?","Because correctness is checkable: evals + retrieval grounding give you measurable, auditable answers � a raw chatbot gives you vibes."],
  ["STAR without numbers scores how?","Mid � interviewers hear 'adjectives'; quantified results (pass-rate 78?92%, $0.008/query) make the story memorable and verifiable."],
  ["What's the worst failure mode in a mock LLM-system-design interview?","Jumping to components before requirements � always ask: users, scale, freshness, latency budget, failure tolerance first."],
  ["What are the three negotiation levers beyond base salary?","Equity/grants, level (affects everything after), and sign-on/relocation � plus travel % and on-call expectations for FDE."],
  ["What do you do with interview feedback within 10 minutes?","Debrief-log it: questions asked, what landed, one concrete fix � compounding reps are the whole point of this phase."],
  ["Why is 'I don't know, but here's how I'd find out' a strong answer?","It signals calibrated confidence � FDE and AI roles both punish bluffing harder than ignorance."],
  ["What's your 90-second flagship pitch formula?","Problem (for whom) ? what you built ? the number that proves it ? one lesson. No architecture jargon."],
  ["Why is sleep non-negotiable before interview day?","Recall and composure degrade sharply on sleep debt; interviews are cognitive performance, and 8h is part of the prep."],
  ["What are the last 5 things to do on interview day?","Pitch �3 aloud, logistics ready, quiet room, water/phone away, and 2 deep breaths before you enter."]
];

const GLOSSARY = [
  ["LLM","Large Language Model � a neural network trained to predict the next token over massive text corpora"],
  ["Token","The atomic text unit models read/write � roughly � of a word in English"],
  ["Context window","The maximum text (tokens) the model can attend to in one call"],
  ["Temperature","Sampling randomness: 0 = near-deterministic, higher = more creative"],
  ["System prompt","Permanent instruction defining the assistant's role, rules and output format"],
  ["Few-shot prompting","Providing input?output examples so the model infers the pattern"],
  ["Chain-of-thought","Prompting the model to reason step-by-step before answering"],
  ["Structured output","Constraining model output to a schema (JSON) your code can parse"],
  ["Hallucination","Fluent, confident text that is factually wrong � structural to next-token prediction"],
  ["Embedding","A numeric vector representing text meaning; similar texts = nearby vectors"],
  ["Cosine similarity","Similarity between embeddings measured by angle; 1 = identical meaning"],
  ["Vector database","Storage + ANN (approximate nearest neighbor) search over embeddings"],
  ["RAG","Retrieval-Augmented Generation � retrieving relevant docs and stuffing them into the prompt as evidence"],
  ["Chunking","Splitting documents into retrievable pieces; strategy changes everything"],
  ["BM25","Classic keyword ranking (term frequency � inverse document frequency)"],
  ["Hybrid search","Combining dense (vector) and sparse (keyword) retrieval for best recall"],
  ["Re-ranker","A cross-encoder that re-scores query?chunk pairs precisely after cheap retrieval"],
  ["HyDE","Hypothetical Document Embeddings � generate a fake answer, embed it, search with it"],
  ["Golden set","The fixed, hand-labeled test set every change must pass"],
  ["Recall@k","Fraction of queries where the correct chunk appears in the top k results"],
  ["LLM-as-judge","Using an LLM with a rubric to score outputs � must be calibrated to humans"],
  ["Faithfulness","Does the answer stick to the retrieved context, or invent beyond it?"],
  ["Agent","An LLM in a loop with tools, a budget, and a stop condition"],
  ["ReAct","Reason + act loop pattern: think, call a tool, observe, repeat"],
  ["Tool calling","The model emitting a structured request to invoke an external function"],
  ["MCP","Model Context Protocol � open standard connecting agents to any tool/data server"],
  ["Guardrails","Hard limits: max iterations, token/cost caps, timeouts, approval gates"],
  ["Human-in-the-loop","Pausing the agent for human approval before side-effecting actions"],
  ["Idempotency","An operation safe to retry � same request never produces duplicate effects"],
  ["Prompt injection","Malicious instructions smuggled into data/retrieved content to hijack the agent"],
  ["Semantic cache","Caching answers keyed by embedding similarity of queries"],
  ["Model routing","Sending requests to the cheapest capable model; escalating on failure"],
  ["SLO","Service Level Objective � an explicit target (p95 latency, error rate, eval score)"],
  ["FDE","Forward Deployed Engineer � customer-embedded engineer owning end-to-end delivery"],
  ["SOW","Statement of Work � scope, acceptance criteria, timeline and price in writing"],
  ["Autonomy ladder","Suggest ? draft ? act with approval ? act autonomously; earned in stages"],
  ["Rollout","pilot ? canary ? full-scale release with rollback at each stage"],
  ["STAR","Behavioral answer framework: Situation, Task, Action, Result"],
  ["P95 latency","95% of requests complete faster than this � the honest tail-latency metric"],
  ["Tracing","Recording every step of a run (prompts, tools, tokens, latency) for debugging"],
  ["Drift","Quality decay over time as data/models/users change � caught by evals, not vibes"]
];

window.SPRINT_DATA.QUIZ = QUIZ;
window.SPRINT_DATA.GLOSSARY = GLOSSARY;
